<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Judicate of Cagliari</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1258 CE (approx.) · capital Santa Igia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Southern Sardinian kingdom ruled by native judges from Santa Igia until Pisa destroyed it in 1258.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Judicate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, also called Calari, took shape in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in the 10th and 11th centuries as Byzantine authority lapsed. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-igia">Santa Igia</a>, near the lagoon west of modern Cagliari, and its judges came from the Lacon-Gunale family, who alternated the names Torchitorio and Salusio.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its judges ruled the largest of the four Sardinian realms, granting land and churches to Italian and French monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Montecassino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-victor-marseille">Saint-Victor of Marseille</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> competed for influence; in 1216 the Pisans built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a> on the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> with Judge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetta-of-cagliari">Benedetta</a>'s reluctant consent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the last judge, William III, allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisans</a> and their Sardinian allies destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-igia">Santa Igia</a> in 1258. The judicate was divided among Pisa, Arborea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a> and the Pisan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-gherardesca">della Gherardesca</a> family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a> grew into the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/judicate-of-cagliari.jpg" alt="Judicate of Cagliari" width="320" height="626"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judicate_of_Cagliari_(XI-XIV_centuries).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1258 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Igia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Benedetta of Cagliari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sardinian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Giudicato di Cagliari, Kingdom of Calari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1129&amp;focus=judicate-of-cagliari" aria-label="Judicate of Cagliari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4906 772 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Judicate of Cagliari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5226 1028L5709 970M5226 1028L5250 884"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5250 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 1028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1129&amp;focus=judicate-of-cagliari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
